{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>העירה</b> — Both are verbs that are not transitive; or העירה would be transitive, the sense being \"Your very self,\" like קודר הלכתי, which is transitive. And because the author joined העירה and הקיצה to משפטי, he joined אלהי. The sense: העירה אלהי למשפטי, and הקיצה אדני לריבי. And in my opinion: because You are Elohim and You are Adonai — the sense is \"my Lord\" — for it is written with alef and dalet."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :register {:flags {0 ["Lord"]}}, :notes {0 "The word יוצא/אינו יוצא refers to whether the verb governs a direct object (transitive). The phrase עצמך כדרך קודר הלכתי appears to cite a verse form where the pronominal suffix is attached directly to the verb; rendered literally as found. The final comment about alef and dalet clarifies that אדני is pointed as 'my Lord' (with a 1cs suffix) rather than as the divine title Adonai."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:29:45.808040509Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}